Heterostructured materials (HSMs) exhibit remarkable mechanical properties arising from the interaction between their soft and hard zones (Fig.  2 ). These zones differ significantly in flow stress, leading to a heterogeneous response to applied strain [ 19 ]. Soft zones (indicated in green color in Fig.  2 ), typically coarse-grained (micrometric sized), undergo larger deformations, while hard zones (indicated in blue color in Fig.  2 ), often nano- or ultrafine-grained, act as barriers to dislocation movement, enhancing material strength [ 26 ]. Interfaces between these zones, also known as zone boundaries, are crucial to accommodate strain mismatches by generating geometrically necessary dislocations (GNDs) [ 27 ]. Figure 2 Representation of the three deformation stages (I, II, and III) of heterostructured materials together with the dislocation behavior and stress distribution (black line) from the zone boundary (red line).  τ a \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
